# Go Object Oriented Design

Go is a thoroughly object-oriented language. It allows
methods to be defined <mark>on any type</mark>, without the boxing/unboxing common to other languages.

There is no familiar `class` based system, in particular, Go [does not provide inheritance][composition]. What one must understand is that classical inheritance and object-oriented are not synonymous.

> "Use of classical inheritance is always optional; every problem that it solves can be solved another way." - [Sandi Metz][poodr]

Much of what classes offer can be found in Go, just as independent concepts:

* polymorphism via <mark>interfaces</mark>
* automatic message delegation via <mark>embedding</mark>
* namespacing via <mark>exports</mark>

